    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 6817.8,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 826,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, water is much more than a simple chemical formula. It is the defining substance of our planet and the foundation of all known life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at its unique properties, its role on Earth, and why it is so essential.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make it unlike almost any other substance on Earth:\n\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states of matter: \n  * **Liquid** (the oceans, rivers, rain)\n  * **Solid** (ice, snow, glaciers)\n  * **Gas** (water vapor, steam, humidity)\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water is exceptionally good at dissolving other substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals). This is why it can transport vital nutrients through our bloodstreams and through the roots of plants.\n* **Ice Floats:** Most liquids shrink and become denser when they freeze, but water actually *expands* and becomes less dense. This is why ice floats on water. Because of this, lakes and oceans freeze from the top down, allowing marine life to survive the winter underneath the ice.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat without drastically changing its own temperature. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps human bodies from overheating.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up from the roots of a tree to its highest leaves).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nWater covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, mostly in the oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). \n\nDespite how much of it there is, **only about 2.5% is fresh water**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible freshwater in lakes, rivers, and ponds for human use.\n\nWater is constantly moving in a continuous cycle called the **hydrologic cycle** (or water cycle):\n1. The sun heats liquid water, causing it to evaporate into gas (water vapor).\n2. The vapor rises, cools, and condenses into clouds.\n3. The clouds release the water back to the ground as precipitation (rain, snow, sleet).\n4. It flows into bodies of water, and the cycle repeats.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Why Water is Essential for Life\nAs far as scientists know, **life cannot exist without water.** \n* **Biological Habitat:** The first living organisms on Earth originated in the oceans, and all life still depends on an aqueous (water-based) environment to function.\n* **Human Body:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. Our brains are about 73% water, our lungs about 83%, and even our bones are roughly 31% water. \n* **Metabolism:** Water is the medium in which all the cellular and chemical reactions of life take place. It digests food, flushes out waste, lubricates joints, and regulates body temperature.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It sculpts the Earth's landscape, drives our weather and climate, and acts as the literal lifeblood of every organism on the planet."
